{
  // Type checking WITHOUT TypeScript.
  //
  // The site stays JavaScript on purpose: authoring is buildless, View Source
  // shows the same program that runs, and every module here is imported by
  // contract-tests.mjs under plain node. Types erase at build time, so a
  // conversion would buy nothing at runtime — workerd executes identical
  // JavaScript either way. What IS worth having is the CHECKER, which reads the
  // JSDoc already in these files and the generated Workers types, and emits
  // nothing.
  //
  // The scope was cal/src alone when this landed and was meant to GROW; it now
  // covers the site Worker too, which is the whole request path. Booking is
  // still the highest-stakes code here (it moves real meetings, sends real mail,
  // and signs its own approval links), so it stays first in the include.
  //
  // Widening it on 2026-08-10 cost 112 errors, resolved as:
  //
  //   72   one heterogeneous ROUTES table taking its element type from the first
  //        entry, exactly as predicted. Settled by ONE annotation on the new
  //        ROUTE_TABLE declaration in src/worker/index.ts.
  //   18   serendipity/serendipity.js, which the glob never names — tsc follows
  //        imports, so widening to the Worker drags in everything it reaches.
  //   22   scattered, and all of one family: a value that coerces at runtime and
  //        does not typecheck. Date subtraction, boolean subtraction, isNaN(Date),
  //        Response.redirect(URL), searchParams.get() into a numeric param.
  //
  // NONE of the 112 was a live bug, same as the 2026-08-07 measurement. That is
  // still the honest case for this check: it is here to catch the NEXT one, of
  // the shape where a property is read that the object never had and the value
  // silently becomes 0 or undefined, which no test in this repo would notice.
  //
  // The coercion family is worth a note, because "harmless at runtime" is what
  // makes it worth fixing rather than suppressing. Every one of those 22 now says
  // its units out loud (.getTime(), Number(), .href), which is the same argument
  // the original yw() fix made.
  //
  // What this check can see is narrower than it looks, and it has not changed:
  // in a .js file an unannotated object literal stays expandable, so `x.nope` is
  // NOT an error. The checker enforces the JSDoc you write. Widening the include
  // buys the CALL-SITE and inference errors above; it does not retro-fit shapes
  // onto 90 unannotated modules, and nobody should read a green run as if it had.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "target": "esnext",
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    // .ts import specifiers, which is FORCED rather than stylistic. Measured
    // 2026-08-16: bun resolves both ./x.js and ./x.ts to x.ts, wrangler/esbuild
    // resolves both, and node 26 resolves ONLY ./x.ts — a .js specifier naming a
    // .ts file throws. Keeping plain node able to import these modules is the
    // whole point of gotcha 16, so the specifier has to say .ts and this flag is
    // what lets tsc agree. It requires noEmit, which is already set above.
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    // Workers globals only. With the DOM lib loaded, its CacheStorage shadows
    // Cloudflare's and every `caches.default` reads as an error.
    "lib": ["esnext"],
    "types": ["@cloudflare/workers-types"],
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    // Not `strict`. The goal is finding real mistakes in working JavaScript, not
    // relitigating every implicit any in a codebase that was never annotated.
    "strict": false
  },
  // Paths are relative to THIS file, which lives in config/ — hence the `../`.
  "include": ["../cal/src/**/*.js", "../src/worker/**/*.ts"]
}
